When a large habitat is broken into small patches:
- mammals and birds that need **large territories**, and migratory animals, are badly affected, so the populations of large animals decline (ii);
- many species disappear from the small patch, so **biodiversity decreases** (iii);
- a small patch has a long edge relative to its area, so species from the surrounding habitats, including invasive ones, enter it and **competition from surrounding habitats increases** (iv).

Statement (i) is wrong: small, disturbed patches are more open to invasive species, not free of them. So the answer is option (A) (ii), (iii) and (iv) only.

(a) Darwin's theory of natural selection **could not explain how the variations arose** in a population and how they are passed on, because the mechanism of inheritance was not known in his time. Darwin also took evolution to be gradual, through small variations, whereas Hugo de Vries, working on the evening primrose, showed that large differences arising suddenly (mutations, single-step large mutation or saltation) also cause evolution.

(b) The most accepted theory today is the **synthetic theory of evolution (the modern synthesis)**. It states that the origin of species is based on the **interaction of genetic variation and natural selection**: genetic variation (from mutation, gene recombination and gene flow), genetic drift and natural selection change the gene frequencies of a population, and reproductive isolation finally leads to new species.

(c) Modern biology explains the source of variation, which Darwin could not, in these ways:
- **Mutation**: random changes in the genetic material produce new alleles;
- **Recombination** during meiosis: crossing over and independent assortment give new combinations of genes;
- **Sexual reproduction and hybridisation**, which bring together genes from two parents.

(i) A **plasmid** is an autonomously replicating, extra-chromosomal circular DNA found in bacteria. It is an important tool because:
- it can act as a **vector**: a piece of foreign DNA linked to it is carried into the host cell;
- it has an **origin of replication (ori)**, so it multiplies within the host along with the linked DNA, giving many copies;
- it carries **selectable markers** that help to pick out the transformed cells;
- it is small and has unique restriction sites, which makes insertion of the foreign DNA easy.

Plasmids can be isolated from bacteria such as **Escherichia coli**, **Agrobacterium tumefaciens** (the Ti plasmid) and **Salmonella typhimurium** (whose plasmid Cohen and Boyer used for the first recombinant DNA). Any two sources are enough.

(ii) **'ori'** is the sequence from where replication starts. Any piece of DNA linked to this sequence can be made to replicate within the host cells, and ori is also responsible for controlling the **copy number** of the linked DNA, so to get many copies of the target DNA it is cloned in a vector whose ori supports a high copy number.
A **selectable marker** helps in identifying and eliminating the **non-transformants** and selectively permitting the growth of the **transformants**. Genes encoding resistance to antibiotics such as ampicillin, chloramphenicol, tetracycline or kanamycin are the usual selectable markers for E. coli: only cells that have taken up the vector grow on a medium containing that antibiotic.

(iii) Recombinant DNA is made by cutting the vector and the foreign DNA and joining them, and the cutting is done by **restriction endonucleases**. Each restriction endonuclease recognises a specific **palindromic nucleotide sequence** (for example EcoRI recognises GAATTC) and cuts both strands at specific points within it, a little away from the centre, leaving single-stranded overhanging stretches called **sticky ends**. When the vector and the foreign DNA are cut with the same enzyme, they carry the same kind of sticky ends, which pair by hydrogen bonding and are then sealed by DNA ligase to form the recombinant DNA. Without a restriction endonuclease the DNA cannot be cut at a precise site, so the recombinant molecule cannot be built.

(i) In the diagram of the human ovum:
- a: the cells of the **corona radiata**, follicle cells surrounding the ovum;
- b: the **zona pellucida**, the layer just outside the plasma membrane of the ovum (the perivitelline space was also accepted);
- c: the **haploid nucleus** of the ovum.

(ii) The ovum is released from the ovary as a **secondary oocyte**, before its second meiotic division is complete. The division is completed:
- **when**: once the sperm enters the cytoplasm of the ovum, i.e. at fertilisation;
- **where**: in the **fallopian tube**, at the ampullary region, where fertilisation takes place;
- **how**: the entry of the sperm induces the completion of the **second meiotic division** of the secondary oocyte. The division is unequal and gives a small **second polar body** and a large haploid **ovum (ootid)**. The haploid nuclei of the sperm and the ovum then fuse to form the diploid zygote.

(iii) During fertilisation, a sperm comes in contact with the **zona pellucida** layer of the ovum and **induces changes in the membrane** that **block the entry of additional sperms**. This ensures that only one sperm can fertilise an ovum.
